---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const problems = [
	{
		label: 'clarity',
		line: 'A good idea that only works as long as you’re the one explaining it.',
	},
	{
		label: 'systems',
		line: "A process that only works because it's stuck in one person's head.",
	},
	{
		label: 'communication',
		line: "Someone excellent at the work who can't yet explain it clearly enough for someone else to do it.",
	},
];
---

<BaseLayout title="Brandon DeVere, Business Systems Consultant" description="Brandon DeVere is a Business Systems Consultant who turns knowledge trapped in one person into documented systems that let businesses grow without them.">
	<section class="container section home">
		<div class="hero">
			<h1>I build what makes growth possible without you having to be in every room.</h1>
			<p class="subhead">I'm a Business Systems Consultant. I turn knowledge trapped in one person into documented systems that scale without them.</p>
			<p>
				Most organizations hit the same wall eventually: the person who understands the business best becomes
				the bottleneck, because every new hire, every process, every decision keeps routing back through one
				person's head. I find that knowledge and turn it into something documented and reusable, so growth
				doesn't depend on any one person repeating themselves forever.
			</p>
			<p>
				I've done this in hotel operations, real estate, recruiting leadership, and now in AI-assisted
				documentation. Different industries, different tools, same method every time: find the knowledge that
				only exists because one person keeps repeating it, then build the system that replaces the repetition.
			</p>
		</div>

		<ul class="problems">
			{problems.map((p) => (
				<li>
					<p class="meta">{p.label}</p>
					<p>{p.line}</p>
				</li>
			))}
		</ul>

		<div class="next">
			<p>
				The case studies below show this same pattern in two different industries: a documented onboarding
				system that let three real estate organizations grow past 900 people combined, and a documentation
				system that fixed a broken content process using AI. See how in <a href="/work">Work</a>.
			</p>
			<p>
				Curious how a concierge desk, a real estate brokerage, and AI documentation all became the same skill?
				That's the <a href="/about">About</a> page.
			</p>
			<p>
				If you just want the background that led here, the <a href="/resume">Resume</a> has it.
			</p>
		</div>
	</section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
	.hero h1 {
		max-width: 20ch;
		font-size: clamp(1.75rem, 4vw, 2.5rem);
	}

	.subhead {
		color: var(--color-ink-muted);
		font-size: 1.125rem;
	}

	.problems {
		list-style: none;
		margin: var(--space-2xl) 0 0;
		padding: 0;
		display: grid;
		grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr);
		gap: var(--space-lg);
	}

	.problems li p:last-child {
		max-width: none;
	}

	@media (max-width: 768px) {
		.problems {
			grid-template-columns: 1fr;
			gap: var(--space-md);
		}
	}

	.next {
		margin-top: var(--space-2xl);
	}

	.next p {
		margin-bottom: var(--space-md);
	}
</style>
